⏺ This reframe is the right one, and it's sharper than a compromise — it dissolves the where/when tension entirely instead of splitting the difference. Let me reflect on it in the four
  directions you asked, grounded in what Palimpsest already is.

  1. The shift in framing — and why it's architecturally cheap

  The move is from pre-processing pipeline (imperative, fixed, hidden inside self_similarity, one-shot) to pre-analysis analytics (declarative, plural, user-run, persisted, visualizable).
  The crucial consequence: chunking and embedding stop being steps and become analyses that emit reusable layers.

  What makes this strong rather than aspirational is that Palimpsest's annotation-layer machinery already is the substrate for this. A track is precisely "a named, parameterized,
  provenance-stamped layer of offset-anchored records with a manifest." So:

  - a chunk layer is a track whose records are {index, start, end, text, words} — it already exists as a return value; the reframe just promotes it from an ephemeral local in
  self_similarity to a first-class persisted track.
  - an embedding layer is a track keyed to a chunk layer + model, whose records carry vectors (or vector refs).

  So you're not building a new subsystem — you're routing chunking/embedding through the rails you just hardened (G1 params, G3 provenance/atomicity, the OffsetMap remap, the contract-lock
  pattern, the elements-ribbon UI idiom).

  ★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────
  - The reframe collapses three problems into one solved one. "Where/when to invoke" (it's user-run like any analysis), "how to make it reusable" (it's a persisted track), and "how to keep
  it honest" (it gets the same declare→enforce→lock + provenance as the substrate) all reduce to "a chunk/embedding layer is a track."
  - It also fixes the coupling smell directly: a second embedding-based track today would duplicate self_similarity's invocation. Once embeddings are a layer, the second track declares a
  dependency on an embedding layer instead of re-invoking — and that dependency is checkable (model, dim, chunk-layer identity), which is exactly the "build checks into analyses that have
  dependencies" you described.
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

  The one non-negotiable that survives unchanged: chunk output gets the contract-lock (in-bounds in analyzable coords, index-sequential, ordered, partition for non-overlapping modes) — it's
   the only substrate-boundary producer still trusted rather than validated. That's the no-regret first step whenever you're ready, independent of everything below.

  2. What it looks like for the user — methods, params, visualizations, combinations

  Chunking as an analysis ("how is this text segmentable?"). The user picks a method, sets params, runs → a named chunk layer. The information it reveals:
  - chunk-length distribution (words & chars), count, overlap ratio, coverage;
  - boundary alignment — what fraction of chunk edges fall on sentence / paragraph / verse / element boundaries (a real signal about the text's natural granularity; "smart" mode already
  implicitly chases this);
  - a chunk ribbon rendered over the text (the same barcode idiom as elements/verses), so segmentation is seen, not just counted.

  Methods worth exposing beyond today's word/slide/punctuation/verse/smart: paragraph- and sentence-unit, fixed-char, regex-delimited, and eventually content-aware boundaries
  (TextTiling-style cohesion dips). Each is a methodology the user compares, not a default they inherit.

  Embedding as an analysis ("what does the semantic space look like?"). Pick a chunk layer + model (MLX Qwen3-4B @2560, or alternative dims/models) + params, run → an embedding layer keyed
  to (chunk-layer, model, dim). The information it reveals:
  - a 2-D/3-D projection (PCA/UMAP/t-SNE) of selected/filtered chunks, colorable by structure (book/chapter/verse) or by another layer (sentiment/topic);
  - nearest-neighbor explorer, similarity heatmap, outlier/cluster surfacing;
  - filtering before embedding: "only Genesis," "only chapters," "chunks matching a query."

  Exploring combinations — concrete vignettes:
  - Granularity sweep: chunk word-7 vs slide-10 vs smart-paragraph-50, lay their ribbons over each other, see which respects the text's structure → pick one to feed self_similarity.
  - Representation compare: embed the same chunk layer with two models, project both, see whether the semantic geography is model-stable before trusting any downstream similarity result.
  - Filtered semantics: filter to footnotes only → embed → project colored by which chapter they annotate → discover whether apparatus clusters by content or by position.

  The dependency model that makes reuse safe: each chunk layer carries a small typed capability descriptor (mode, overlapping?, covers-full-text?, unit). A downstream analysis declares what
   it needs and the system reuses a compatible layer or refuses with a clear reason — never silently consuming an incompatible one (the anti-Goodhart, fail-loud posture from the audit).

  3. What else belongs in the Wave-0 layer

  The unifying definition: Wave 0 = "know your text, and validate it, before you model it" — the EDA + data-quality + representational-foundation stage. Three families beyond
  chunking/embedding:

  (a) Descriptive & distributional statistics (mostly auto-computed, cheap):
  - counts: tokens, types, vocabulary size, hapax; lexical diversity (TTR, MATTR, MTLD, Yule's K);
  - length distributions: word/sentence/paragraph/chunk (histogram + ECDF + by-element-type violins);
  - frequency structure: rank-frequency Zipf fit (log-log + slope), Heaps' vocab-growth curve, function-word profile (the stylometric base), n-gram tables, stopword ratio;
  - readability indices as descriptive signals (not normative scores).

  (b) Data-quality / integrity QC (this is where your contract-lock pays a second dividend):
  - a user-facing integrity report that runs the validators I just built (masking partition, segment anchoring, section forest, OffsetMap round-trip, analyzable↔original length) and shows
  green/violations — "is this text's coordinate substrate sound?";
  - encoding/normalization sanity (non-ASCII, control chars, mojibake), language / mixed-language detection;
  - segmentation sanity (degenerate or oversized paragraphs), near-duplicate / repeat-passage finder (surface the repeat-masking signal as a report), masking-coverage summary (% masked by
  type), structural-count summary.

  (c) Positional / lexical exploration (interactive, layer-producing):
  - KWIC / concordance, dispersion plots (where a term lives across the doc — a lexical barcode), collocations (PMI / log-likelihood), burstiness.

  Statistical tests/visuals that fit honestly: histograms/KDE/ECDF, box/violin by element type, QQ plots, Zipf/Heaps fits, and light exploratory tests (KS comparing two sections' length
  distributions; chi-square on category counts) — always framed descriptive-of-this-text, with explicit caveats when there's no reference corpus to be inferential against (consistent with
  the §5 honesty pass).

  4. The Stage-1 Analysis Suite / control-center

  A workbench shown right after import/mask, for a text or subtext, organized around layers as the currency:

  - Header: text identity + a substrate-integrity badge (one click runs the contract validators as a report) — the first thing you see is "are my coordinates sound?"
  - Profile tab (auto, cheap, on open): the descriptive-stats dashboard + distribution thumbnails + data-quality flags. The "at-a-glance know-your-text."
  - Representations tab (your chunking + embedding workbench): produce / inspect / compare chunk and embedding layers; chunk ribbon over the text; embedding-space projection; a layer
  manager listing every layer with its provenance and capability descriptor.
  - Explore tab (interactive proto-analyses): KWIC, dispersion, collocations, duplicate finder — lightweight, each producing a layer.
  - Composition affordances threaded throughout: "use this layer in →" hand-off to a Wave-1 analysis; "compare layers"; "filter → embed → project." Every run also emits a printable report
  (feeding the documentary tier), so the suite is exploratory and on-the-record.

  Principles tying it back to the rails you've built: every layer is parameterized + fail-loud + provenance-stamped (G1/G2/G3); coordinate-anchored and contract-locked (this walk); plural
  and explicit (no hidden one-size preprocessing); honestly visualized (descriptive-not-inferential, no faked progress). The suite is essentially "the contract-locked substrate, made
  legible and interactive, with chunks and embeddings as its first two optional analytic layers."

  ---
  That's the reflection. Where I'd suggest going next, in increasing commitment — and I'll wait for your steer:

  1. No-regret now: land the chunk-output contract-lock (the (A) we both agree on) — small, isolating, brings chunking under the same regime as the other five pieces.
  2. Then the structural move: promote chunk (and embedding) from self_similarity-locals to first-class layer tracks with capability descriptors + dependency checks — the decoupling.
  3. Then the suite: the Profile/Representations/Explore workbench + the broader Wave-0 analytics.











