  strings. These 'chunks' might or might not be overlapping, but whatever layer of a text has been 'chunked', all characters within that layer belong to at least one 'chunk'. In this way
  we could approximate chunking into sentences, verses, paragraphs, clauses, strings of a given word-length, strings of a given character-length, and etc. 'Chunks' can be compared against
  each other to assess 'similarity'. 'Similarity' is defined as some variation on the concept of 'distance' between two chunks. 'Distance' could be the minimum edit distance between the
  two strings that are the 'chunks'. 'Distance' could be the vector-distance between two 'chunks' which have been embedded using a common embeddings model. 'Distance' could be a measure
  of the difference in syntactical structure of the strings in two 'chunks'. 'Distance' could be the difference in score between two 'chunks' for any other kind of NLP analysis like
  sentiment, topic and any and ALL other NLP or computational linguistic analysis or metric that can be applied to a contiguous string aka a 'chunk'. Assume that A and B are not the same
  text but that they have been 'chunked' in a similar way (even non-similar 'chunking' should resolve to 'chunks' that can be compared for 'similarity' even though this might not pass
  rigorous statistical or experimental design assumptions, e.g. text A 'chunks' derived on string-length of 10 words and text B 'chunks' derived on string-length of 7 words) and that
  their set of 'chunks' has been subjected to the same post-chunking analysis (e.g. model embeddings or sentiment analysis). There will be M 'chunks' from text A and N 'chunks' from text
  B, each 'chunk' having some value of the same kind of metric assigned to it. Irrespective of how the chunks were formed we can generate an MxN matrix where each element of the matrix is
  a vector of length 2, such that element ij is (Mi, Nj). From there we can derive a matrix whose elements are (Mi-Nj). In some cases, a 'chunk' is not assigned a metric value Mi or Nj,
  but instead the derived matrix is generated by directly calculating a metric value such as 'edit distance', which is still conceptually congruent with any other kind of (Mi-Nj)
  matrix-element representation we are talking about. This derived (Mi-Nj) matrix is a 'distance matrix' and it can be visualized easily in the Palimpsest UI. Two texts can be snalyzed in
  this way to produce a variety of 'distance matrices', with the only caveat being that all elements of that derived 'distance matrix' (Mi-Nj) are the result of logical operations (i.e.
  Mi and Nj have to be the same kind of metric, or (Mi-Nj) itself is a metric value derived from a comparison of M versus N). If the texts are both text A, then this entire analytical process belongs in a single-text 'Project' including the ability to execute all of thes operations and review the results as summary statistics and visualizations in
